+- [ ] T017 [US1] Implement the email alternative in `src/logredact/redact.py` as named group `email`, exactly `(?<![A-Za-z0-9._%+-])[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9-]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9-]+)*\.[A-Za-z]{2,}`: local part one or more of letters, digits and `. _ % + -`; exactly one `@`; dot-separated letter/digit/hyphen labels; last label at least two letters; **at least two labels** (R6). The leading negative lookbehind is **required for linear-time matching** (SR-005) and must not be removed — without it the pattern is quadratic and one 1 MiB line takes an extrapolated ~1.5 hours (FR-004, research.md R5)
+- [ ] T018 [US1] Implement the IPv4 alternative in `src/logredact/redact.py` as named group `ip`: `(?<![0-9.])` + `OCTET` + `(?:\.OCTET){3}` + `(?![0-9.])`, where `OCTET = (?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1[0-9][0-9]|[1-9][0-9]|[0-9])` — four dot-separated octets each 0–255 with no leading zeros beyond a single `0`, and neither end flanked by a digit or a dot. The octet branches are mutually exclusive, so the alternative is linear by construction (FR-005, FR-013, SR-005)
+- [ ] T019 [US1] Implement the card alternative in `src/logredact/redact.py` as named group `card`: `(?<!\d)\d+(?:[ -]\d+)*`, matching a **maximal** run of digit groups joined by single spaces or single hyphens. Doubled separators deliberately do not match `[ -]`, which is what leaves `4111  1111  1111  1111` and `4111--1111--1111--1111` alone (FR-006, research.md R8)
+- [ ] T020 [US1] Combine T017–T019 into one module-level compiled pattern in `src/logredact/redact.py`, written in precedence order `(?P<email>…)|(?P<ip>…)|(?P<card>…)`. The alternation order **is** the precedence; there is no enum, registry, or rule table (FR-014, A-2, data-model.md Redaction kind, research.md R4)
+- [ ] T021 [US1] Implement card candidate selection in `src/logredact/redact.py`: within a matched run, a candidate is a contiguous sequence of **whole groups** whose total digit count is **13 to 19**, whose internal separators are all the **same** single space or single hyphen, and whose digits pass Luhn. Select **leftmost first, longest at that position**, replace with `[CARD]`, continue scanning after the replaced candidate, and reproduce every character of the run that is not part of a redacted candidate unchanged. Use the incremental two-parity Luhn sum in **O(1) extra memory** — appending a digit flips the doubling parity of every earlier digit, so `A' = B + digit` and `B' = A + doubled(digit)`, with `doubled(d) = 2*d - 9 if 2*d > 9 else 2*d`. Do **not** tokenise the run or join candidate strings: that design measured 15.8 s per adversarial 1 MiB line and allocated ~500,000 strings, breaking SC-010 (FR-006, FR-007, FR-008, FR-013, SR-005, SC-010, research.md R8)
+- [ ] T022 [US1] Implement the replacement callback and `redact_line(text, tally) -> str` in `src/logredact/redact.py` using `pattern.sub(replacer, text)`: dispatch on `match.lastgroup` (`email`, `ip`, `card`), return the fixed placeholder, and increment the matching tally key once per replacement. `re.sub`'s own semantics deliver FR-014 — leftmost match, non-overlapping, alternatives tried in written order, replaced text never re-scanned — so add no bookkeeping of your own. Accumulate no match list, so memory stays bounded by one line (FR-014, FR-016, A-1, research.md R4)
+- [ ] T023 [US1] Implement the standard-input line loop in `src/logredact/cli.py`: reconfigure `sys.stdin` to `encoding="utf-8", errors="surrogateescape", newline=""` and read with `stream.readline(MAX_LINE_CHARS + 1)` until it returns `""`. A chunk shorter than `MAX_LINE_CHARS + 1` is a complete line; read standard input only when there are no operands (FR-002, FR-016, SR-005, research.md R2, R3)
